  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R1, R2, R3, R8, R14, R16, R18, and R19 establish mechanical/high-energy alloying of equimolar elemental HEA powders followed by rapid SPS consolidation, including use of argon, short milling intervals in some cases, vacuum SPS, phase evolution, refinement, and densification. R1 and R4 explicitly connect fine or nanocrystalline HEA structures to grain-boundary and solid-solution strengthening, while R2 and R16 show that milling time controls refinement, homogeneity, phase fractions, hardness, and contamination. Most importantly, R7 and R10 identify Ti's large atomic size as a strong influence on phase formation and mechanical strength in closely related Co/Cr/Fe/Ni-containing HEAs. Although no record gives the exact CoCrFeNiTi composition, 1–6 h milling, 900–1000 °C SPS schedule, or the precise 83% FCC/17% BCC outcome, an expert could combine the established MA/HEBM–SPS route with the known Ti-driven phase and strengthening effects to propose the later intervention and mechanism."
